const CONTEXT = `Repo: /home/lane/MythosMini — a 1,050,241-param byte-level (vocab 257) looped-shared-block transformer trained on fineweb-edu, benchmarked on WikiText-2 byte PPL, BLiMP, ARC-Easy against the Tiny-ML leaderboard (overall = mean of blimp, arc, normalized wiki score; efficiency = overall/log10(params+1) so PARAMETER GROWTH IS PENALIZED only logarithmically — doubling params costs ~5% efficiency but any overall gain counts fully). Current pipeline: procedural Dyck warmup -> 2B-token training (NAdamW, WSD schedule) -> 3-round recursive STM -> benchmark all checkpoints -> SLERP tournament -> SFT instruct phase -> instruct SLERP. Architecture: 4 prelude + 1 shared block looped 2-6x with per-loop LoRA + 4 coda, dim 96, RMSNorm/RoPE/SwiGLU, tied embeddings, DSA-style sparse attention (shared model-level lightning indexer, top-128 keys, 64 local window, FlexAttention short path, chunked long path, 1M max context), FP4 E2M1 QAT on all linears. Recent regression: moving the indexer from per-attention to one shared model-level indexer + detaching the indexer bias for prelude/coda blocks raised throughput 0.85->1.19 Mtok/s but the verification run scored 59.47 overall vs 60.40 before, AND its STM round 3 NaN-diverged (prior runs round 3 gave +0.57; NaN also seen once before with output-embedding centering). All-time best: 60.41 (recursive STM, commit accd37b). GPU: one RTX 5090 32GB, torch 2.10.0+cu128. Known: full-model torch.compile broken (inductor dtype bug + flex recompile issues), STM phase runs at only ~76 ktok/s vs training 1.19 Mtok/s. USER GOAL: push quality as far as possible toward Gemma-class output quality (text in/text out) — ambition is the point; consider parameter growth, distillation, anything that fits one 5090.`

const results = await parallel([
  () => agent(`${CONTEXT}\n\nRead /home/lane/MythosMini/journal.md IN FULL and /home/lane/MythosMini/scores.md. Extract every technique that was tried with its measured outcome. Produce findings: which proven-good techniques are NOT currently in the default pipeline (or were weakened), which failed approaches must not be retried, and what the data says about where the 59.47 vs 60.40 regression came from. Be specific about numbers.`, { label: 'history-miner', phase: 'Analyze', schema: FINDINGS }),
  () => agent(`${CONTEXT}\n\nRead /home/lane/MythosMini/src/mythosmini/model/transformer.py, src/mythosmini/training/loop.py, src/mythosmini/training/stm.py, src/mythosmini/training/optimizer.py, src/mythosmini/training/scheduler.py, src/mythosmini/config.py. Hunt for QUALITY bugs and theory problems: (1) is the shared model-level indexer (selection computed from embedding output, reused by all 9 blocks incl. prelude/coda with detached bias) theoretically weaker than per-layer indexers, and how to get quality back cheaply? (2) What plausibly causes STM round-3 NaN at constant lr 3e-4 on FP4+sparse weights (look at the detached-bias gradient path, FP4 STE, the indexer gate growth, missing eps/clamps)? Propose concrete fixes with exact code locations. (3) Any other quality-harming detail (init, lr, schedule, loop sampling, tied embedding scale).`, { label: 'code-auditor', phase: 'Analyze', schema: FINDINGS }),
  () => agent(`${CONTEXT}\n\nResearch via web search: state-of-the-art techniques (2024-2026) for maximizing quality of SUB-10M-parameter language models. Focus on: hyperparameter scaling laws for tiny models (muP, depth/width, lr vs size — note current peak_lr 3e-3 for dim 96), modern optimizers (Muon, AdamW-variants, schedule-free), data curriculum beyond simple filtering (TinyStories-style synthetic data for tiny models — huge documented wins at <10M params), repeated-epoch vs fresh-data tradeoffs at 2B tokens for 1M params (extreme overtraining — what do papers say), and embedding/softmax tricks for byte-level models. Each finding must be implementable on one RTX 5090 in this codebase.`, { label: 'research-tiny-lm', phase: 'Analyze', schema: FINDINGS }),
  () => agent(`${CONTEXT}\n\nResearch via web search: knowledge distillation from a larger open teacher into a tiny student (2024-2026 best practice). We have one RTX 5090 32GB; teacher candidates: Qwen3-0.6B, SmolLM2-135M/360M, Gemma-3-270M, any small model with open logits. The student is BYTE-LEVEL (vocab 257) while teachers use BPE — research vocabulary-mismatch distillation (universal logit distillation, byte-level alignment, generating synthetic training TEXT from the teacher instead of logit matching). Estimate: can we generate/distill enough high-quality synthetic text (TinyStories showed GPT-3.5-generated data made 1-10M models coherent) on this GPU in hours, and what quality gain is documented at ~1M params? Also research SmallTalk/BabyLM-winner recipes (BabyLM challenge winners' exact techniques at 10-100M words). Concrete, implementable findings only.`, { label: 'research-distill', phase: 'Analyze', schema: FINDINGS }),
  () => agent(`${CONTEXT}\n\nRead /home/lane/MythosMini/src/mythosmini/training/stm.py and src/mythosmini/benchmarks/*.py and src/mythosmini/merging/tournament.py. The STM phase runs at ~76 ktok/s while main training hits 1.19 Mtok/s — find out why (per-sample loss with reduction=none, fixed loops=4, micro 128 after OOM shrink, flex recompiles from varying batch shapes — note dynamo recompile limit 8 with dynamic=False compile of flex_attention, the final partial batch shape). Propose concrete speedups with exact code changes. Also check benchmark/tournament phases for waste. Target: STM phase at >=500 ktok/s.`, { label: 'stm-speed', phase: 'Analyze', schema: FINDINGS }),
  () => agent(`${CONTEXT}\n\nRead /home/lane/MythosMini/train.py, src/mythosmini/hardware/autotune.py, src/mythosmini/training/loop.py, src/mythosmini/model/transformer.py. Training currently 1.19 Mtok/s at micro 64 (autotuned; dense model used to autotune to 256 and hit 2.4 Mtok/s). Find remaining throughput: why does autotune now pick 64 (O(n^2) bool selection mask at batch 256 = 256*512*512 bools + topk buffers?), can selection construction be cheaper (block-sparse mask at flex BLOCK_SIZE granularity instead of per-element, bitpacking, bf16 scores), is the flex backward avoidable for detached-bias blocks (mask_mod instead of score_mod has much cheaper backward), grad_accum tradeoffs. Also evaluate: should ModelConfig grow (dim 128-192, more heads) given efficiency only penalizes log10(params) — what dim maximizes overall score per wall-clock hour at ~1.2 Mtok/s? Propose exact changes.`, { label: 'train-speed-scale', phase: 'Analyze', schema: FINDINGS }),
])

phase('Verify')
const all = results.filter(Boolean).flatMap((r, i) => (r.findings || []).map(f => ({ ...f, src: ['history', 'code', 'tiny-lm', 'distill', 'stm-speed', 'train-scale'][i] })))
log(`${all.length} raw findings collected`)
const verified = await parallel(all.map(f => () =>
  agent(`${CONTEXT}\n\nAdversarially review this proposal for the MythosMini codebase. Read the actual code it touches before judging. Refute it if: already tried and failed per journal.md, theoretically unsound at ~1M params, won't fit one RTX 5090, conflicts with the leaderboard benchmark setup (byte-level, fixed eval), or the expected gain is wishful. PROPOSAL [from ${f.src}]: ${f.title} — ${f.detail} — expected: ${f.expected_gain} — risk: ${f.risk}`, { label: `verify:${f.title.slice(0, 30)}`, phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICT })
    .then(v => ({ ...f, verdict: v }))
))
const sound = verified.filter(Boolean).filter(f => f.verdict?.is_sound)
log(`${sound.length}/${all.length} findings survived adversarial review`)

phase('Synthesize')
const plan = await agent(`${CONTEXT}\n\nYou are the lead architect. Here are adversarially-verified findings for maximizing MythosMini quality (recover 59.47->60.4+, then push as far beyond as possible toward Gemma-class coherence) plus throughput. Produce a final ordered implementation plan: max 10 changes, ordered by (expected gain / effort), each with exact files, exact change description, and how to verify. Mark each QUALITY vs SPEED vs DATA. Respect the repo rule: one training-behavior feature per 2B verification run — group into at most 3 run-batches. If synthetic-data generation (TinyStories-style) survived review, include the exact generation recipe (teacher model, prompt template, volume, hours on the 5090). FINDINGS:\n${JSON.stringify(sound, null, 1)}`, { label: 'synthesizer', phase: 'Synthesize' })
return { plan, soundCount: sound.length, totalCount: all.length }
